    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/6/66360.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 25,
    "turn_text": "I want the country to be united. But the highest value in our constitutional republic is the rule of law. We are governed by laws and we're governed by the processes prescribed by those laws. We are not governed by the passions of the mob. And the highest value here is accountability. There has to be consequences for committing sedition against the United States Congress in an attempt to derail the peaceful transfer of power. If we do not have the peaceful transfer of power, if we do not have a separation of powers, then there's nothing left that we can claim is a democracy or republic. The best hope for unity in the long run is Joe Biden. He is as moderating and as unifying a force in American politics as you could hope for. But I'm pessimistic about the prospects for unity, not only because of the siege, but there are forces driving the radicalization of the political right in particular, not only social media, not only Twitter, but this Fox News, there's talk radio, there's Donald Trump, there's demagogues in Congress, there's an ecosystem, a whole ecosystem of alternate media outlets that keep in flaming the disaffected supporters of Donald Trump. and keep beating them inflammatory lies and false claims of election fraud. And as long as that reality persists, it's hard to see a path to a unified America. It's hard to see the kind of unity that took hold in the wake of my alum.",
